## Why a CLI instead of an MCP server

An MCP server loads its tool definitions into the agent's context in every session, and each result travels back through the model before the agent can act on it. A CLI costs nothing until it is called. The agent learns it from a short skill, filters the output with `jq` or `grep`, and chains several steps in one shell script, for example turning this week's events into reminders in a single run.

`__BIN__` is one of five CLIs with the same output format, exit codes and permission model: [apple-mail](https://github.com/Bam679/apple-mail-cli), [apple-notes](https://github.com/Bam679/apple-notes-cli), [apple-calendar](https://github.com/Bam679/apple-calendar-cli), [apple-reminders](https://github.com/Bam679/apple-reminders-cli) and [paperless](https://github.com/Bam679/paperless-cli). Together they let an agent manage everyday things on a Mac with one set of rules. If your agent cannot run shell commands, an MCP server is the better fit.

## Install

1. Install the CLI:

   ```sh
   brew install Bam679/tap/__BIN__
   ```

   Signed release archives and `go install` are described in [How to install and verify a release](docs/how-to/install-and-verify.md).

2. Check that it can reach __TARGET__:

   ```sh
   __BIN__ doctor
   ```

   __FIRST_RUN__ See [Security and permissions](#security-and-permissions).

3. Connect Claude Code. The plugin adds a skill that teaches the agent the CLI, and a hook that stops the agent from editing the policy file:

   ```sh
   claude plugin marketplace add Bam679/__REPO__
   claude plugin install __BIN__@__REPO__
   ```

   To get all five tools and the shared `agent-cli` skill from one place, use the [agent-cli-plugins](https://github.com/Bam679/agent-cli-plugins) marketplace instead. Install each plugin from one marketplace only.

To remove everything: `claude plugin uninstall __BIN__`, `brew uninstall __BIN__`, and delete `~/.config/agent-cli/__BIN__.json` if you created one.

